                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to compare the teeth of a beaver and a Smilodon in terms of size, shape, and function.\n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n- Beavers are large, semiaquatic rodents known for their ability to build dams and lodges. They have sharp, strong teeth that are adapted for gnawing on wood.\n- Smilodon, also known as the saber-toothed cat, was a prehistoric carnivorous mammal that lived during the Pleistocene epoch. It had long, curved, and sharp canine teeth that were used for hunting and killing prey.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) What are the size differences between a beaver's teeth and a Smilodon's teeth?\n2) What are the shape differences between a beaver's teeth and a Smilodon's teeth?\n3) What are the functional differences between a beaver's teeth and a Smilodon's teeth?\n\nAnswers to sub-questions:\n1) A beaver's teeth are much smaller than a Smilodon's teeth. A beaver's incisors can grow up to 1 inch (2.5 cm) long, while a Smilodon's canine teeth could reach up to 11 inches (28 cm) in length.\n2) A beaver's teeth are chisel-shaped, with a sharp edge for cutting through wood. A Smilodon's teeth are long, curved, and dagger-like, designed for stabbing and slicing through flesh.\n3) A beaver's teeth are primarily used for gnawing on wood to build dams and lodges, as well as for eating plant material. A Smilodon's teeth were used for hunting and killing prey, as well as for tearing and consuming meat.\n\nBased on the differences in size, shape, and function, the answer to the original question is:\n\nB) False - A beaver's teeth would not rival that of a Smilodon.",
