                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to consider the process of embalming and its effects on the body.\n\nEmbalming is a process that involves preserving a deceased body by injecting chemicals, such as formaldehyde, into the body's tissues. This process helps to slow down the decomposition of the body and allows for a more natural appearance during a funeral service.\n\nNow, let's consider how an embalmed body might feel different at a funeral compared to a non-embalmed body. \n\n1. Rigidity: Embalming can cause the body to become more rigid due to the chemicals used in the process. This can make the body feel firmer to the touch compared to a non-embalmed body.\n\n2. Temperature: An embalmed body will likely be cooler to the touch than a non-embalmed body, as the embalming process removes much of the body's natural heat.\n\n3. Texture: The chemicals used in embalming can cause the skin to become more firm and less pliable, which may result in a different texture when touched.\n\nBased on these factors, we can conclude that embalmed bodies do feel different at funerals compared to non-embalmed bodies. Therefore, the answer is:\n\nA) True",
                        "answers": [
