                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: Is it true that embalmed bodies feel different at funerals?\n\nTo answer this question, let's consider the embalming process and its effects on the body.\n\nEmbalming is a process that involves preserving a deceased body by injecting chemicals, such as formaldehyde, into the body's tissues. This process helps to slow down the decomposition process and allows the body to be displayed at a funeral.\n\nNow, let's think about how this process might affect the way a body feels.\n\n1. The embalming process replaces bodily fluids with preservative chemicals, which can cause the body to feel firmer and less pliable than it would naturally.\n\n2. The process also involves massaging the body to relieve rigor mortis (the stiffening of the muscles after death), which can make the body feel more relaxed and less stiff than it would without embalming.\n\n3. Additionally, the body may be dressed and made up for the funeral, which can alter the way it feels to the touch, such as the texture of the skin or the softness of the hair.\n\nBased on these factors, it is reasonable to conclude that embalmed bodies do feel different at funerals compared to non-embalmed bodies.\n\nAnswer: A) True",
                        "answers": [
