                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to understand the origin of the term \"Homer\" in baseball and its relation to the poet Homer who wrote the Odyssey.\n\nAdditional facts:\n1. In baseball, a \"Homer\" is short for \"home run,\" which is a hit that allows the batter to circle all the bases and score a run.\n2. Homer is an ancient Greek poet, known for writing the epic poems the Iliad and the Odyssey.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the origin of the term \"home run\" in baseball?\n2. Is there any connection between the term \"Homer\" in baseball and the poet Homer?\n\nAnswers to sub-questions:\n1. The term \"home run\" in baseball originated in the late 19th century and was used to describe a hit that allowed the batter to run around all the bases and score a run without any errors or assistance from the opposing team. The term \"Homer\" is simply a shortened version of \"home run.\"\n2. There is no connection between the term \"Homer\" in baseball and the poet Homer. The term \"Homer\" in baseball is derived from the term \"home run\" and has no relation to the ancient Greek poet.\n\nAnswer to the original question:\nB) False. A \"Homer\" in baseball is not named after the poet Homer who wrote the Odyssey. It is a shortened version of the term \"home run\" and has no connection to the ancient Greek poet.",
