                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, what does the term \"light year\" mean?\n\nProfessor: A light year refers to the distance that light travels in one year.\n\nStudent: So, does that mean it's a measure of time or distance?\n\nProfessor: It's a measure of distance. Specifically, it's the distance that light can travel in one year.\n\nStudent: How far is that exactly?\n\nProfessor: Well, the speed of light is about 186,000 miles per second. So, in one year, light can travel about 5.88 trillion miles.\n\nStudent: Wow, that's a really long distance! But why do we use light years instead of just miles or kilometers?\n\nProfessor: We use light years because it's a more convenient way to measure distances in space. The universe is so vast that using regular units of distance like miles or kilometers would be impractical. Plus, light years give us a sense of how long it takes for light to travel from one point to another in space.\n\nStudent: I see. So, if we wanted to measure the distance between two stars, for example, we would use light years?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. We would measure the distance in light years because it gives us a better sense of the scale of the universe.",
