                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A \"light year\" refers to the distance light travels in one year. To answer this question, we need to understand the concept of a light year and the options provided.\n\nA light year is a unit of distance used in astronomy to describe the vast distances between celestial objects. It is defined as the distance that light travels in a vacuum in one Julian year (365.25 days). Since light travels at a constant speed of approximately 299,792 kilometers per second (km/s) or 186,282 miles per second (mi/s), we can calculate the distance of a light year.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the speed of light in a vacuum?\n2. How many seconds are in a Julian year?\n3. How can we calculate the distance of a light year?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The speed of light in a vacuum is approximately 299,792 km/s or 186,282 mi/s.\n2. There are 31,557,600 seconds in a Julian year (365.25 days * 24 hours/day * 60 minutes/hour * 60 seconds/minute).\n3. To calculate the distance of a light year, we multiply the speed of light by the number of seconds in a Julian year: 299,792 km/s * 31,557,600 s = 9.4607 x 10^12 km.\n\nNow, let's analyze the options provided:\n\nA) the distance light travels in one year: This is the correct definition of a light year, as we have established through our sub-questions and answers.\n\nB) the time it takes light to travel from Earth to the Sun: This is not correct. The time it takes light to travel from the Sun to Earth is approximately 8 minutes and 20 seconds, which is much shorter than a year.\n\nC) the amount of light required on Earth in one year: This is not correct. A light year is a unit of distance, not a measure of the amount of light needed on Earth.\n\nD) the distance between two sources of light: This is not correct. A light year is a specific unit of distance based on the distance light travels in one year, not a general term for the distance between two light sources.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) the distance light travels in one year.",
