                    {
                        "id": "2fbf19b1-10b9-4082-96bb-6c199687a38a",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, let's first understand the role of gravity in the formation of stars.\n\nStars form from clouds of gas and dust in space. Gravity plays a crucial role in this process by pulling the gas and dust particles together. As these particles come closer, their gravitational attraction increases, causing them to clump together and form a dense core. This core continues to grow as more and more material is attracted to it. Eventually, the core becomes so massive and dense that the pressure and temperature inside it rise to the point where nuclear fusion can occur. This is when a star is born.\n\nNow, let's analyze each statement to see which one best describes the role of gravity in the formation of stars:\n\nA) Gravity converts solid matter into gases and light energy.\n- This statement is incorrect. Gravity does not convert solid matter into gases and light energy. Instead, it pulls gas and dust particles together to form a dense core, which eventually leads to the birth of a star.\n\nB) Gravity causes gases and dust particles to condense into spheres.\n- This statement is correct. Gravity pulls gas and dust particles together, causing them to condense into a dense core. As more material is attracted to the core, it eventually forms a sphere due to gravity's equal pull in all directions. This spherical core is the precursor to a star.\n\nC) Gravity cools gases and liquids until they become one solid mass.\n- This statement is incorrect. Gravity does not cool gases and liquids; instead, it pulls them together, increasing their density and temperature. The increased temperature and pressure eventually lead to nuclear fusion, which creates a star.\n\nD) Gravity pushes rocks and dust particles outward from a dense center.\n- This statement is incorrect. Gravity does not push rocks and dust particles outward from a dense center. Instead, it pulls them together, causing them to form a dense core that eventually leads to the birth of a star.\n\nBased on our analysis, the correct answer is:\n\nB) Gravity causes gases and dust particles to condense into spheres.",
                        "answers": [
