                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " \n\nThe first step in star formation is the collapse of a molecular cloud. So we need a force that can cause collapse, and that force is gravity. Gravity is also responsible for the second step, which is the formation of a protostar. A protostar is a dense, hot ball of gas that forms at the center of the collapsing cloud. So gravity is responsible for both the collapse of the cloud and the formation of the protostar. \n\nThe third step is the formation of the star itself, and this is where things get a little more complicated. The star forms when the protostar ignites and starts to produce energy through nuclear fusion. The force that ignites the star is not gravity, but the force that keeps the star from collapsing under its own weight is gravity. So while gravity is not responsible for the formation of the star, it is responsible for keeping the star from collapsing. \n\nThe fourth and final step is the formation of the planetary system. Gravity is responsible for this as well, by causing the dust and gas around the star to condense into planets. \n\nSo to answer the question, gravity is responsible for the formation of stars, but not for the ignition of the star itself.",
