                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question: What is the primary cause of the differences in water levels at the beach, sometimes reaching the pier and sometimes barely covering the pillars under the pier?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step:\n\n1. We know that the differences in water levels at the beach are due to tides.\n2. Tides are caused by the gravitational forces of celestial bodies.\n3. The main celestial bodies that influence Earth's tides are the Sun and the Moon.\n4. Asteroids and comets have negligible effects on Earth's tides due to their relatively small mass and distance from Earth.\n\nComparing the options:\n\nA) The Sun does have an influence on Earth's tides, but its effect is less than that of the Moon due to the greater distance between the Earth and the Sun.\n\nB) The Moon has the most significant influence on Earth's tides because it is closer to Earth, and its gravitational force has a stronger effect on the water levels.\n\nC) Asteroids have a negligible effect on Earth's tides.\n\nD) Comets also have a negligible effect on Earth's tides.\n\nBased on our reasoning, the primary cause of the differences in water levels at the beach is the gravitational influence of the Moon (option B).",
