                        "cot": "\n\nStudent: Professor, I noticed that when I go to the beach, the water rises as high as the pier at some times, but at other times of the day, the water barely covers the pillars under the pier. What causes these differences in water level?\n\nProfessor: The differences in water level are primarily due to the gravitational influence of the Moon.\n\nStudent: Why does the Moon have a gravitational influence on the water level?\n\nProfessor: The Moon's gravity pulls on the water, causing the water to rise. When the Moon is high in the sky, its gravity pulls more strongly on the water, and so the water level is higher. When the Moon is low in the sky, its gravity is not as strong, and so the water level is lower.",
