                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The rock cycle is the process by which rocks are formed, broken down, and reformed over time. There are three main types of rocks: igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic.\n\nAdditional fact: Igneous rocks are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is an igneous rock?\n2. How are igneous rocks formed?\n3. What is the process of cooling and solidification?\n4. What is magma and lava?\n\nAnswers:\n1. An igneous rock is a type of rock that is formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava.\n2. Igneous rocks are formed when magma or lava cools and solidifies. This can happen either on the surface of the Earth (as with lava flows) or beneath the surface (as with magma chambers).\n3. Cooling and solidification is the process by which a liquid (in this case, magma or lava) changes into a solid. As the magma or lava cools, its atoms and molecules slow down and begin to arrange themselves into a crystalline structure.\n4. Magma is a molten rock that is found beneath the Earth's surface. Lava is magma that has erupted onto the Earth's surface.",
