                    {
                        "id": "d4ff27d2-b03c-41f9-bfe2-33c2b7c10f0a",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question: What causes the formation of an igneous rock in the rock cycle?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step:\n\n1. The rock cycle is a series of processes that continuously change rocks from one type to another. There are three main types of rocks: igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic.\n\n2. Igneous rocks form from the cooling and solidification of molten material called magma or lava. Magma is formed when temperatures are high enough to melt rock, and it rises to the Earth's surface due to its lower density compared to the surrounding solid rock.\n\n3. Let's analyze each option:\n\nA) Cooling temperatures: This option is consistent with the formation of igneous rocks, as they form when magma or lava cools and solidifies.\n\nB) High pressure and heat: This option is more related to the formation of metamorphic rocks, which form when existing rocks are subjected to high pressure and heat, causing them to change their mineral composition and structure.\n\nC) Weathering and erosion: This option is more related to the formation of sedimentary rocks, which form from the accumulation and compaction of sediments produced by weathering and erosion of pre-existing rocks.\n\nD) Compacted plant remains: This option is also related to the formation of sedimentary rocks, specifically coal, which forms from the compaction of plant remains over millions of years.\n\nBased on our analysis, the correct answer is:\n\nA) Cooling temperatures",
                        "answers": [
