                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding the rock cycle and how igneous rocks form. Can you help me?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's start by looking at the options given. Do you know what each of these processes entails?\n\nStudent: I think I have a general idea, but I'm not sure which one applies to igneous rocks.\n\nProfessor: Alright, let's go through them one by one. Option A is cooling temperatures. What do you think happens when temperatures cool in the context of rocks?\n\nStudent: I guess the molten rock would solidify?\n\nProfessor: Exactly! Now, let's move on to option B, which is high pressure and heat. What type of rock do you think forms under these conditions?\n\nStudent: I think that's how metamorphic rocks form, right?\n\nProfessor: Correct! Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks are subjected to high pressure and heat, causing them to change. Now, let's look at option C, weathering and erosion. What do you think this process does to rocks?\n\nStudent: It breaks them down into smaller pieces?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's right. Weathering and erosion break down rocks into smaller particles, which can eventually form sedimentary rocks. Finally, option D is compacted plant remains. What type of rock do you think forms from this process?\n\nStudent: I think that's how some sedimentary rocks form, like coal.\n\nProfessor: Correct again! So, now that we've gone through all the options, which one do you think applies to the formation of igneous rocks?\n\nStudent: I think it's option A, cooling temperatures.\n\nProfessor: That's right! Igneous rocks form when molten rock, or magma, cools and solidifies. Well done! Do you have any other questions about the rock cycle?\n\nStudent: No, I think I understand it better now. Thank you, Professor!",
