                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, let's first understand what happens during a volcanic eruption. When a volcano erupts, it releases molten rock called magma from deep within the Earth's crust. This magma reaches the surface and is called lava. As the lava flows and cools, it solidifies and forms rocks on the surface of the volcano.\n\nNow let's analyze each option:\n\nA) Rock cools quickly from melted rock: This option is consistent with the process described above, as the lava cools and solidifies to form rocks on the surface of the volcano.\n\nB) Rock is recrystallized by extreme pressure: This process is more related to the formation of metamorphic rocks, which are formed when existing rocks are subjected to high pressure and temperature, causing them to recrystallize. This is not the primary process for surface rock formation on volcanoes.\n\nC) Rock solidifies slowly deep underground: This process describes the formation of intrusive igneous rocks, which form when magma cools and solidifies slowly beneath the Earth's surface. While this process is related to volcanic activity, it is not the major process for surface rock formation on volcanoes.\n\nD) Rock is formed from deposited sediment: This process describes the formation of sedimentary rocks, which form from the accumulation and compaction of sediments. This is not the primary process for surface rock formation on volcanoes.\n\nBased on the analysis above, the correct answer is:\n\nA) Rock cools quickly from melted rock.",
