                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to understand the process of rock formation on volcanoes and identify which of the given options best describes that process. \n\nVolcanoes are formed by the movement of tectonic plates, which causes magma (molten rock) to rise from the Earth's mantle to the surface. When this magma reaches the surface, it cools and solidifies, forming volcanic rocks. \n\nNow, let's analyze each option:\n\nA) Rock cools quickly from melted rock.\nThis option describes the process of magma cooling and solidifying on the surface of a volcano. This is a plausible answer, as it aligns with the process of volcanic rock formation.\n\nB) Rock is recrystallized by extreme pressure.\nThis option refers to the process of metamorphic rock formation, which occurs when existing rocks are subjected to high pressure and temperature, causing them to recrystallize. This is not the primary process of rock formation on volcanoes.\n\nC) Rock solidifies slowly deep underground.\nThis option describes the formation of intrusive igneous rocks, which form when magma cools and solidifies below the Earth's surface. While this process is related to volcanic activity, it is not the primary process of rock formation on the surface of volcanoes.\n\nD) Rock is formed from deposited sediment.\nThis option refers to the formation of sedimentary rocks, which form from the accumulation and compaction of sediments. This process is not directly related to volcanic activity and is not the primary process of rock formation on volcanoes.\n\nBased on our analysis, the major process of surface rock formation on volcanoes is:\n\nA) Rock cools quickly from melted rock.",
