                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding the major process of surface rock formation on volcanoes. Can you help me?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's look at the options given. Do you know what each of these processes entails?\n\nStudent: I think I have a general idea, but I'm not sure which one applies to volcanoes.\n\nProfessor: Alright, let's go through them one by one. Option A says that rock cools quickly from melted rock. This process is called solidification, and it occurs when molten rock, or magma, cools and hardens. Does this sound like it could be related to volcanoes?\n\nStudent: Yes, that makes sense because volcanoes have magma.\n\nProfessor: Exactly. Now let's look at option B. Rock is recrystallized by extreme pressure. This process is called metamorphism, and it occurs when existing rock is subjected to high pressure and temperature, causing the minerals within the rock to recrystallize. Do you think this is related to volcanoes?\n\nStudent: I'm not sure. It could be, but I think the first option seems more likely.\n\nProfessor: Good thinking. Let's move on to option C. Rock solidifies slowly deep underground. This process is called plutonism, and it occurs when magma cools and solidifies slowly beneath the Earth's surface, forming large bodies of intrusive igneous rock. Does this sound like it could be related to volcanoes?\n\nStudent: It seems related to magma, but I'm not sure if it's related to surface rock formation on volcanoes.\n\nProfessor: You're right. While plutonism is related to magma, it doesn't directly involve the formation of surface rocks on volcanoes. Finally, let's look at option D. Rock is formed from deposited sediment. This process is called sedimentation, and it occurs when sediment accumulates and compacts over time, forming sedimentary rock. Does this sound like it could be related to volcanoes?\n\nStudent: No, I don't think so. That seems more related to rivers and oceans.\n\nProfessor: Correct! So, based on our discussion, which option do you think is the major process of surface rock formation on volcanoes?\n\nStudent: I think it's option A, where rock cools quickly from melted rock.\n\nProfessor: That's right! The major process of surface rock formation on volcanoes is the solidification of magma, which cools quickly and forms igneous rocks. Good job!",
