                    {
                        "id": "63e2065f-83a2-4904-bb87-1521286998cf",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Sedimentary rocks are formed from the accumulation and cementation of sediment. Sediment can be made up of various materials such as sand, mud, and organic matter. \n\nSub-questions:\n1. What happens to sediment after it is deposited on the ocean floor?\n2. How does sediment become compacted and cemented to form sedimentary rocks?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1: \nAfter sediment is deposited on the ocean floor, it can be buried by additional layers of sediment. Over time, the weight of the overlying sediment can cause the lower layers to become compacted.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2:\nAs sediment becomes buried and compacted, the pressure and weight of the overlying sediment can cause the grains to become tightly packed together. This process is called lithification. Additionally, minerals can precipitate out of groundwater and fill in the spaces between the grains, cementing them together.\n\nAnswering the original question:\nThe next step in the formation of sedimentary rocks after a layer of sediment is deposited on the ocean floor is burial and lithification, where the sediment becomes compacted and cemented together to form rock. Therefore, the answer is B) burying.",
                        "answers": [
