                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The Permian period ended approximately 252 million years ago and is known for the largest mass extinction in Earth's history, where up to 96% of marine species and 70% of terrestrial species went extinct. Evidence suggests that extensive glaciation occurred during this time.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is glaciation?\n2. How could glaciation disrupt ocean ecosystems?\n3. What effect could disrupt ocean ecosystems by producing a steep drop in ocean salinity and density?\n4. What effect could disrupt ocean ecosystems by producing a steep drop in ocean volume and area?\n5. What effect could disrupt ocean ecosystems by increasing exposure to ultraviolet radiation?\n6. What effect could disrupt ocean ecosystems by increasing exposure of tsunamis to ocean life?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Glaciation is the process of the formation, movement, and melting of glaciers.\n2. Glaciation could disrupt ocean ecosystems by causing sea level changes, altering ocean currents, and changing ocean chemistry.\n3. A steep drop in ocean salinity and density could disrupt ocean ecosystems by affecting the ability of organisms to regulate their internal salt and water balance.\n4. A steep drop in ocean volume and area could disrupt ocean ecosystems by reducing habitat availability and altering ocean currents.\n5. Increased exposure to ultraviolet radiation could disrupt ocean ecosystems by damaging DNA and harming photosynthetic organisms.\n6. Increased exposure of tsunamis to ocean life could disrupt ocean ecosystems by causing physical damage to organisms and altering habitat availability. \n\nAnswer: A) A steep drop in ocean salinity and density could disrupt ocean ecosystems by affecting the ability of organisms to regulate their internal salt and water balance.",
                        "answers": [
