                    {
                        "id": "66d4c5a0-f415-45f7-a2b5-297721a29b3c",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain why cold air at the tops of mountains flows toward areas of lower pressure in valleys?\n\nProfessor: Sure. When air is cold, it becomes denser and heavier. This means that it exerts more pressure on the ground. So, when cold air is at the top of a mountain, it exerts more pressure on the ground than the warmer air in the valley below. This creates a pressure gradient, which causes the cold air to flow downhill toward the lower pressure in the valley.\n\nStudent: Does this mean that warm air always rises and cold air always sinks?\n\nProfessor: Not necessarily. It's true that warm air is less dense than cold air, so it tends to rise. But there are other factors that can influence the movement of air, such as pressure gradients and the rotation of the Earth.\n\nStudent: What about the option C, that cold air is considered to be less dense than warmer air?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. Cold air is denser than warm air, which means that it contains more molecules per unit of volume. This is why cold air sinks and warm air rises. So, option C is a true statement.\n\nStudent: And what about option D, that cold air is free of oxygen atoms?\n\nProfessor: That's not true. Cold air contains the same amount of oxygen as warm air. The temperature of the air doesn't affect the amount of oxygen in it. So, option D is incorrect.\n\nProfessor: Do you have any other questions about this topic?\n\nStudent: No, I think I understand it now. Thank you for explaining it to me.",
                        "answers": [
