                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question to ensure we understand it correctly: What happens to cold air at the tops of mountains in relation to air pressure and density?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step:\n\n1. Cold air is denser than warm air. This is because the molecules in cold air are closer together, making it heavier and denser.\n\n2. Air flows from areas of high pressure to areas of low pressure. This is due to the pressure gradient force, which causes air to move from high-pressure areas to low-pressure areas in an attempt to equalize the pressure.\n\n3. In general, air pressure decreases with altitude. This means that the air pressure at the tops of mountains is lower than the air pressure in valleys.\n\nNow, let's evaluate each answer choice:\n\nA) Cold air will flow toward areas of lower pressure in valleys: This statement is true. As we established earlier, air flows from high-pressure areas to low-pressure areas, and the air pressure at the tops of mountains is lower than in valleys. Therefore, cold air will flow from the mountain tops down into the valleys.\n\nB) Cold air will flow toward areas of higher pressure in valleys: This statement is false. As we established earlier, air flows from high-pressure areas to low-pressure areas, not the other way around.\n\nC) Cold air is considered to be less dense than warmer air: This statement is false. As we established earlier, cold air is denser than warm air because its molecules are closer together.\n\nD) Cold air is considered to be free of oxygen atoms: This statement is false. Cold air still contains oxygen atoms, just like warm air. The density and temperature of the air do not affect the presence of oxygen atoms.\n\nBased on our analysis, the correct answer is:\n\nA) Cold air will flow toward areas of lower pressure in valleys.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "6e92d9bf-725c-4fa2-861c-dfa2f1cb8039",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Cold air will flow toward areas of lower pressure in valleys.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "A",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/04/13 07:22:43",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
                        "model": "{'name': 'gpt-4', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
                        "comment": "",
                        "annotations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "422743c5-3569-4761-b937-9afcdcd4fa8e",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The original question asks about the behavior of cold air at the tops of mountains. We know that cold air is denser than warm air, and that air generally flows from areas of high pressure to areas of low pressure.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) Does cold air create areas of high or low pressure?\n2) How does air pressure change with altitude?\n3) How does air pressure relate to air flow?\n\nAnswers to sub-questions:\n1) Cold air is denser than warm air, which means it creates areas of high pressure.\n2) Air pressure decreases with altitude, so there is generally lower pressure at the tops of mountains compared to valleys.\n3) Air flows from areas of high pressure to areas of low pressure.\n\nAnswer to the original question:\nSince cold air creates areas of high pressure and air flows from high to low pressure, the cold air at the tops of mountains will flow toward areas of lower pressure in valleys. Therefore, the correct answer is A) will flow toward areas of lower pressure in valleys.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "2b24aac6-5afa-4b45-8254-5744adcc2e3f",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
