                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Forest fires are often caused by a combination of factors, including dry weather conditions, high temperatures, and human activity. However, one of the most common natural causes of forest fires is lightning strikes.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is a forest fire?\n2. What weather conditions are conducive to forest fires?\n3. What are the common causes of forest fires?\n4. Which of the given weather events is most likely to cause a forest fire?\n\nAnswers:\n1. A forest fire is an uncontrolled fire that occurs in a forest or other wooded area.\n2. Forest fires are more likely to occur during hot, dry weather conditions when there is little to no rainfall.\n3. Forest fires can be caused by natural factors such as lightning strikes, as well as human activity such as campfires, cigarettes, and fireworks.\n4. Based on the helpful knowledge and sub-questions, the most likely weather event to cause a forest fire is a lightning strike (option B). While flash floods, tornadoes, and hailstorms can cause damage to forests, they are not typically associated with starting fires.",
