                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: Which weather event has the highest probability of causing a forest fire?\nOptions:\nA) a flash flood\nB) a lightning strike\nC) a tornado\nD) a hailstorm\n\nStep 1: Analyze each option and its potential to cause a forest fire.\n\nA) A flash flood involves a large amount of water, which would likely extinguish or prevent fires rather than cause them.\n\nB) A lightning strike can generate a lot of heat and potentially ignite flammable materials in a forest, such as dry leaves and branches.\n\nC) A tornado can cause damage to trees and structures, but it does not typically involve the heat or ignition sources necessary to start a fire.\n\nD) A hailstorm involves ice and cold temperatures, which are not conducive to starting fires.\n\nStep 2: Compare the potential of each option to cause a forest fire.\n\nBased on the analysis in Step 1, a lightning strike (Option B) has the highest potential to cause a forest fire, as it involves heat and ignition sources. The other options involve water, wind, or cold temperatures, which are less likely to start fires.\n\nAnswer: B) a lightning strike",
                        "answers": [
