                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding which weather event is most likely to cause a forest fire. Can you help me work through the options?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's go through each option one by one. First, we have a flash flood. What do you think about that?\n\nStudent: Well, a flash flood involves a lot of water, so I don't think it would cause a forest fire.\n\nProfessor: That's correct. A flash flood would actually help to extinguish a fire rather than cause one. Now, let's consider a lightning strike.\n\nStudent: Lightning is very hot and can cause things to catch fire, so I think it could cause a forest fire.\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's a good point. Lightning can indeed cause forest fires, especially if it strikes a dry area with lots of fuel for the fire. Now, what about a tornado?\n\nStudent: Tornadoes are very destructive, but I'm not sure if they would cause a forest fire.\n\nProfessor: You're right that tornadoes are destructive, but they typically don't cause fires. They can uproot trees and cause damage, but they don't generate the heat needed to start a fire. Finally, let's look at a hailstorm.\n\nStudent: Hail is made of ice, so I don't think it would cause a forest fire.\n\nProfessor: Correct again! Hailstorms can cause damage to trees and other structures, but they don't have the heat necessary to start a fire. So, based on our discussion, which weather event do you think is most likely to cause a forest fire?\n\nStudent: I think a lightning strike is the most likely to cause a forest fire.\n\nProfessor: That's correct! Lightning strikes are the most likely weather event to cause a forest fire among the options given. Good job working through the options!",
                        "answers": [
