                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "When light hits a mirror, most of the light is:\n\nTo answer this question, we need to understand the properties of a mirror and how it interacts with light. A mirror is a smooth, reflective surface that is typically made of glass with a thin layer of metal, such as aluminum or silver, on the back. The purpose of a mirror is to reflect light, allowing us to see our reflection.\n\nNow, let's define the terms in the answer choices:\n\nA) Refracted: Refraction is the bending of light as it passes through a medium with a different refractive index, such as from air to water or glass. This is not the primary interaction between light and a mirror.\n\nB) Reflected: Reflection is the bouncing of light off a surface, such as a mirror. This is the primary interaction between light and a mirror.\n\nC) Absorbed: Absorption is when light is taken in by a material and converted into other forms of energy, such as heat. While some absorption may occur in a mirror, it is not the primary interaction between light and a mirror.\n\nD) Transmitted: Transmission is when light passes through a material, such as glass or water. This is not the primary interaction between light and a mirror, as the metal layer on the back of the mirror prevents light from passing through.\n\nBased on our understanding of mirrors and the interactions between light and materials, we can conclude that when light hits a mirror, most of the light is:\n\nB) reflected.",
