                    },
                    {
                        "id": "db52f5b8-ac67-4c60-89c0-f3accdd02eb9",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The pitch of a sound wave is determined by its frequency, which is the number of cycles per second. Therefore, we know that both sound waves have the same frequency.\n\nThe loudness of a sound wave is determined by its amplitude, which is the height of the wave. A larger amplitude means a louder sound. Therefore, we can conclude that the amplitude of the second sound wave is greater than the amplitude of the first sound wave.\n\nThe wavelength of a sound wave is the distance between two consecutive peaks or troughs of the wave. It is related to the frequency and speed of the wave by the equation wavelength = speed/frequency. Since we know that the frequency is the same for both sound waves, we can conclude that the wavelength is also the same.\n\nThe speed of sound in air is determined by the temperature, humidity, and pressure of the air. It is a constant value under specific conditions. Therefore, we can conclude that the speed of both sound waves is the same.\n\nIn summary, the property that is greater for the second sound wave is the amplitude (B).",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "fd9718b7-6a8c-4b11-bf28-06c296bba267",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
