                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Recall the definitions of the properties listed in the question:\n- Frequency: the number of cycles of a wave that occur in one second, measured in Hertz (Hz)\n- Amplitude: the maximum displacement of a wave from its equilibrium position, measured in units such as meters or decibels (dB)\n- Wavelength: the distance between two consecutive points on a wave that are in phase (e.g. two peaks or two troughs), measured in units such as meters or centimeters\n- Speed in air: the speed at which sound waves travel through air, which is approximately 343 meters per second at room temperature\n\nStep 2: Identify the property that is the same for both sound waves:\nThe question states that both sound waves have the same pitch. Pitch is related to frequency, so we can conclude that the frequency of both sound waves is the same.\n\nStep 3: Determine which property is greater for the second sound wave:\nThe question states that the second sound wave is louder. Loudness is related to amplitude, so we can conclude that the amplitude of the second sound wave is greater than the amplitude of the first sound wave. Therefore, the answer is B) Amplitude.\n\nStep 4: Check the other properties to confirm that they are not greater for the second sound wave:\n- Frequency: We already established that the frequency is the same for both sound waves.\n- Wavelength: Wavelength is related to frequency and speed, but since the frequency is the same and the speed is constant in air, the wavelength must also be the same for both sound waves.\n- Speed in air: The speed of sound in air is constant at a given temperature, so it is the same for both sound waves.",
