                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to understand what a first-level consumer is and how it fits into the food chain. A first-level consumer, also known as a primary consumer, is an organism that feeds on producers. Producers are organisms that create their own food through the process of photosynthesis, such as plants and algae. In a food chain, primary consumers are herbivores that eat plants, while secondary consumers are carnivores that eat primary consumers, and tertiary consumers are carnivores that eat secondary consumers.\n\nNow, let's analyze each option:\n\nA) Wolf: Wolves are carnivores that primarily eat other animals, such as deer, elk, and rabbits. They are not first-level consumers because they do not feed on producers.\n\nB) Hawk: Hawks are carnivorous birds that prey on other animals, such as rodents, reptiles, and smaller birds. They are not first-level consumers because they do not feed on producers.\n\nC) Carrot: Carrots are plants that produce their own food through photosynthesis. They are not consumers at all; they are producers.\n\nD) Rabbit: Rabbits are herbivores that primarily eat plants, such as grasses, leaves, and vegetables. They are first-level consumers because they feed on producers.\n\nBased on this information, the correct answer is:\n\nD) Rabbit",
                        "answers": [
