                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nA) Neutralization\n\nFirst, it is important to note that vinegar is an acid and ammonia is a base.\n\nSub-question 1: What is an indicator?\nAnswer: An indicator is a substance that changes color when it comes into contact with an acid or a base.\n\nSub-question 2: What does it mean when the color of the indicator changes?\nAnswer: When the color of the indicator changes, it means that it has come into contact with either an acid or a base.\n\nSub-question 3: What happens when an acid and a base are mixed together?\nAnswer: When an acid and a base are mixed together, they neutralize each other.\n\nTherefore, the process that occurred when Robert put two drops of an indicator into vinegar and the color turned red, and then added drops of ammonia solution until the color disappeared, was neutralization.",
                        "answers": [
