                    {
                        "id": "488658d5-0bb9-4be3-94ea-76718244b4f0",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding this question. Can you help me work through it?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's look at each option one by one. First, let's consider option A, mass number. Do you know what mass number is?\n\nStudent: Yes, mass number is the total number of protons and neutrons in an atom.\n\nProfessor: That's correct. Now, do protons and electrons have the same mass number?\n\nStudent: No, they don't. Protons are found in the nucleus and contribute to the mass number, while electrons are found outside the nucleus and do not contribute to the mass number.\n\nProfessor: Exactly. So, option A is not the correct answer. Now let's move on to option B, kinetic energy. Do you know what kinetic energy is?\n\nStudent: Yes, kinetic energy is the energy an object has due to its motion.\n\nProfessor: Correct. Now, do protons and electrons always have the same kinetic energy?\n\nStudent: I'm not sure.\n\nProfessor: That's okay. Kinetic energy depends on both the mass and the velocity of an object. Since protons and electrons have different masses and can have different velocities, their kinetic energies can also be different. So, option B is not the correct answer either.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand. What about option C, sign of charge?\n\nProfessor: Good question. Protons have a positive charge, and electrons have a negative charge. So, do they have the same sign of charge?\n\nStudent: No, they have opposite signs of charge.\n\nProfessor: That's right. So, option C is not the correct answer either. Now let's look at the last option, option D, magnitude of charge. Do you know what that means?\n\nStudent: Yes, magnitude of charge refers to the size or amount of charge an object has.\n\nProfessor: Correct. Now, do protons and electrons have the same magnitude of charge?\n\nStudent: Yes, they do. Protons have a charge of +1, and electrons have a charge of -1. The magnitudes of their charges are equal.\n\nProfessor: That's right! So, the correct answer is option D, magnitude of charge. Protons and electrons always have equal magnitudes of charge, even though their charges have opposite signs. Does that make sense?\n\nStudent: Yes, it does. Thank you for helping me work through the question, Professor!",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "e0165983-66da-476c-9b19-95118671e526",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
