                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Insects have wings that are often very small and intricate, with many details that may be difficult to see with the naked eye.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is a hand lens?\n2. How does a hand lens work?\n3. What are the benefits of using a hand lens for observing small details?\n4. How do the other tools listed (thermometer, balance, graduated cylinder) work, and why would they not be useful for observing insect wings?\n\nAnswers:\n1. A hand lens is a small magnifying glass that can be held in the hand.\n2. A hand lens works by using a convex lens to magnify the object being viewed.\n3. The benefits of using a hand lens for observing small details include increased magnification and clarity, as well as the ability to focus on specific areas of the object.\n4. A thermometer measures temperature and would not be useful for observing insect wings. A balance measures weight and would not be useful for observing insect wings. A graduated cylinder measures volume and would not be useful for observing insect wings.",
