                    {
                        "id": "77c42dc6-bb86-44ec-a812-2bd40f2ab0bd",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, we rephrase the question: What is typically represented along the y-axis on a graph?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step:\n\nA graph usually has two axes: the x-axis (horizontal) and the y-axis (vertical). These axes are used to represent different variables or values.\n\nA) Element symbols: These are typically not placed along the y-axis, as they represent specific elements in chemistry and are not usually used in a graph to represent a variable or value.\n\nB) Negative numbers: While negative numbers can be found along the y-axis, they are not the primary purpose of the y-axis. The y-axis is used to represent a range of values, both positive and negative, for a specific variable.\n\nC) Dependent variables: These are the variables that depend on the independent variables. In a graph, the dependent variable is usually represented along the y-axis, as it changes based on the values of the independent variable along the x-axis.\n\nD) Independent variables: These are the variables that are manipulated or controlled in an experiment or study. They are typically represented along the x-axis, as they are the variables that cause changes in the dependent variable along the y-axis.\n\nBased on our reasoning, the correct answer is:\n\nC) Dependent variables",
