                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to understand the purpose of the y-axis on a graph and the relationship between dependent and independent variables.\n\nThe y-axis on a graph is used to represent the values of a dependent variable. A dependent variable is a variable that depends on the value of another variable, called the independent variable. The independent variable is typically represented along the x-axis.\n\nNow, let's analyze the given options:\n\nA) Element symbols: These are not typically represented on the y-axis, as they are not variables.\n\nB) Negative numbers: While negative numbers can be present on the y-axis, they are not the primary purpose of the y-axis. The y-axis is meant to represent the dependent variable, which can include both positive and negative numbers.\n\nC) Dependent variables: As mentioned earlier, the y-axis is used to represent the values of a dependent variable. This option aligns with the purpose of the y-axis.\n\nD) Independent variables: Independent variables are typically represented along the x-axis, not the y-axis.\n\nBased on the analysis, the most likely option to be placed along the y-axis is:\n\nC) Dependent variables",
