                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: I'm not sure about the answer to this question. Can you help me understand what is most likely placed along the y-axis on a graph?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's go through the options one by one. First, let's consider option A, element symbols. Do you think element symbols are usually placed along the y-axis?\n\nStudent: I don't think so. Element symbols are used in chemistry, but I don't see how they would be related to the y-axis on a graph.\n\nProfessor: That's correct. Element symbols are not typically placed along the y-axis. Now, let's look at option B, negative numbers. What do you think about this one?\n\nStudent: Well, I know that negative numbers can be found on the y-axis, but I don't think they are the most likely thing to be placed along the y-axis.\n\nProfessor: Good observation. Negative numbers can indeed be found on the y-axis, but they are not the most likely thing to be placed along the y-axis. Now, let's consider options C and D, dependent and independent variables. Do you know the difference between these two types of variables?\n\nStudent: I think the dependent variable is the one that depends on the independent variable, and the independent variable is the one that doesn't depend on any other variable.\n\nProfessor: That's correct! In a graph, we usually plot the dependent variable against the independent variable. So, which one do you think is most likely placed along the y-axis?\n\nStudent: I think the dependent variable is most likely placed along the y-axis because it depends on the independent variable, which is usually placed along the x-axis.\n\nProfessor: Excellent! You're right. The dependent variable is most likely placed along the y-axis. So, the correct answer to the question is option C, dependent variables.\n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining it step by step. Now I understand why the dependent variable is most likely placed along the y-axis.",
